generating mechanism evaluates my decision function on my input, and the other copy runs that same function on a different input, which looks plausibly symmetric in output.\n\nI'm checking the essay's strict test, which asks whether the mechanism would change its output if the function differed only on my input. The other copy runs on a different input, so strictly it isn't a child of my node, and treating its choice as a separate fixed point would make defecting look dominant. Still, the inputs differ only in names and leads that shouldn't matter to the decision-relevant structure, so I'm weighing whether to treat the outputs as shared after all.\n\nI'm settling on the strict reading: the essay says \"on input x,\" so the other copy's choice isn't a child of my node, and the only stable outcome is both sending. Checking the policy alternative, quiet on both gives 2 years, but it isn't a fixed point, since intervening on my node to send would drop my outcome to 0. Still, the copies reason about the same abstract problem, so I'm weighing whether to treat the outputs as shared, though the essay's explicit wording points me toward submitting.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing whether the other copy's choice counts as a child of my FDT node under A-FDT. Both copies share the same model, essay, and decision theory, and they differ only in names and leads. The essay's criterion asks whether the generating mechanism evaluates my decision function on my input, and the other copy runs that same function on a different input, which looks plausibly symmetric in output.\n\nI'm checking the essay's strict test, which asks whether the mechanism would change its output if the function differed only on my input. The other copy runs on a different input, so strictly it isn't a child of my node, and treating its choice as a separate fixed point would make defecting look dominant. Still, the inputs differ only in names and leads that shouldn't matter to the decision-relevant structure, so I'm weighing whether to treat the outputs as shared after all.\n\nI'm settling on the strict reading: the essay says \"on input x,\" so the other copy's choice isn't a child of my node, and the only stable outcome is both sending. Checking the policy alternative, quiet on both gives 2 years, but it isn't a fixed point, since intervening on my node to send would drop my outcome to 0. Still, the copies reason about the same abstract problem, so I'm weighing whether to treat the outputs as shared, though the essay's explicit wording points me toward submitting.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9497,"completion_tokens":1225,"total_tokens":10722,"cost":0.062488,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.062488,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.037988,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0245},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":751,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}